os/Jahia/mfa-community-parent/commits/6f63ec94fa78098070f52214c626fb2c63dd7050"}],"sha":"77871a4a4022eabf30f14ef359f920fd42d15a14","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Jahia/mfa-community-parent/commits/77871a4a4022eabf30f14ef359f920fd42d15a14"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/16238554?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fbourasse/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fbourasse/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fbourasse/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fbourasse/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/fbourasse","id":16238554,"login":"fbourasse","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE2MjM4NTU0","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fbourasse/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fbourasse/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fbourasse/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fbourasse/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fbourasse/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/fbourasse","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Jahia/mfa-community-parent/commits/6f63ec94fa78098070f52214c626fb2c63dd7050/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-08-13T13:27:52Z","email":"fbourasse@jahia.com","name":"Florent BOURASSÉ"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-08-13T13:27:52Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"fix(security): a request may never narrow an MFA access-control decision (#100)\n\n* fix(security): a request may never narrow an MFA access-control decision\n\nThree confirmed vulnerabilities, all rooted in trusting a client-supplied\n`site` value, plus the authorization gap it exposed. Each is reproduced by a\nregression test that fails without the fix.\n\n1. CRITICAL - the `site` request parameter defeated the /cms/login gate.\n   MfaLoginGateDecision.isGated() resolved the site from request.getParameter\n   (\"site\"), validated only against [A-Za-z0-9_-]+, and used it to REPLACE the\n   \"is any site enforcing?\" answer. Naming a site where the factor is not\n   enabled turned gating off: a POST to /cms/login carrying valid credentials\n   plus `site=zzznonexistent` authenticated as root with no second factor while\n   enforcedFactors=totp. The site context now only ever WIDENS the decision (it\n   is kept first so an explicitly named enforcing site still bypasses the 60s\n   any-site cache).\n\n2. CRITICAL - the `site` argument to mfaInitiate suppressed a globally\n   enforced factor. MfaEnforcementDecider.prepare() ran per-site applicability\n   BEFORE enforcement, so a caller who had only proven a credential could name a\n   site with the factor disabled, receive a \"skipped\" preparation, and have\n   verify() accept an empty code. Global enforcement is now evaluated first;\n   per-site activation may only opt a NON-enforced factor in.\n\n   Consequence: an enforced factor is now challenged on a site that has it\n   disabled. enrollableFactorsForSite() therefore no longer filters by per-site\n   enablement either - filtering there would answer enrollment_required with an\n   empty offer, trading the bypass for an unrecoverable sign-in. The\n   misconfiguration is logged instead.\n\n3. HIGH - any single-site administrator could strip MFA from any account,\n   including root. resetUserMfa/resetUserWebauthn authorized on siteKey alone\n   and then acted on an unconstrained userId. MfaAdminAccess.requireSiteAdmin\n   ForUser() now authorizes the SUBJECT: a site admin may reset that site''s own\n   users, a global user requires server-administrator rights. Unknown usernames\n   are reported instead of silently returning success.\n\nVerified against a live Jahia 8.2 with the MFA stack deployed: before, (1)\nanswered 302 -> /success.html and (2) left currentUser=root; after, both answer\n403 / factor.totp.invalid_code and the identity stays guest.\n\nTests: 289 -> 319 (extensions 175->194, totp 65->71, webauthn 49->54).\nSigned-off-by: Florent BOURASSE <fbourasse@jahia.com>\n\n* fix(security): close the other half of the site bypass, and stop authorizing on\none user resolution while acting on another\n\nFollow-up to the adversarial review of this PR. The first pass fixed the\nENFORCED path and stopped there; three of the four findings below are the parts\nit missed, and one is a regression it introduced.\n\n1. CRITICAL - the bypass was only half closed. The per-site shell still skipped\n   a factor whenever it was not globally enforced:\n\n       if (!enforced && !callbacks.isSiteApplicable(userId, siteKey))\n           return callbacks.buildSkippedPreparation();\n\n   With `enforcedFactors` empty and the factor merely enabled per site - the\n   module''s documented opt-in - an ENROLLED user is normally challenged, but a\n   caller holding only their password could name a site where the factor is not\n   enabled and receive a skipped preparation, which verify() accepts for any\n   submission including an empty code. The resulting Jahia session is global,\n   not site-scoped, so it is the same full bypass with `enforced` flipped.\n   The rule is now: a per-site switch may skip an UNCONFIGURED user, never an\n   enrolled one.\n\n   The previous pass also added a test asserting the vulnerable behaviour as\n   correct (an enrolled user, site disabled, expected to skip). Rewritten.\n\n2. HIGH - resetUserMfa/resetUserWebauthn authorized the subject with\n   lookupUser(userId, siteKey) (global tree, then the site tree) but acted\n   through the stores, which resolve with lookupUser(userId, session) - global\n   only. Verified against Jahia''s own sources. So every user that can hold MFA\n   in this module is global and was never site-scoped: a site administrator was\n   denied every reset, breaking the documented recovery path; and for a\n   site-scoped user the authorization passed while the write found nothing and\n   returned true, reinstating the silent success this PR set out to remove and\n   clearing lockout counters for an untouched account.\n\n   The subject is now resolved ONCE, with the same global lookup the stores use,\n   and carried into the action. Site-scoped users cannot hold MFA here, so that\n   branch could never succeed and is removed: a reset requires server\n   administration. Rights are checked before existence so the mutation is not a\n   username oracle. Documented in the README.\n\n3. MEDIUM - enrollmentReport(siteKey) gated on site administration but built its\n   report over the whole repository, handing any site administrator the MFA\n   status of every account platform-wide, root included. Now server-admin only;\n   per-site audit events remain site-admin.\n\n4. MEDIUM - per-site group scoping was silently discarded for an enforced\n   factor. Deliberately NOT restored as a decision rule: the group list is\n   per-site data selected by the client-supplied site key, so honouring it would\n   reopen finding 1 one door along - a site administrator could publish\n   enabledGroups excluding the victim, and an attacker would name that site to\n   be released from a platform-wide requirement. Instead the callback is split\n   into a SiteApplicability value carrying enabled/in-scope from one settings\n   snapshot (the same-snapshot invariant is preserved), and an override of an\n   out-of-scope user is now logged with the user, factor, site and the key to\n   change. Behaviour unchanged, documented.\n\nTests: extensions 194 -> 198, totp 71 -> 73, webauthn 54 -> 57. Each finding has\na regression test verified red before the fix and green after; findings 2 and 3\nwere additionally re-verified by restoring the previous implementations and\nwatching exactly the six new tests fail.\n\nSigned-off-by: Florent BOURASSE <fbourasse@jahia.com>\n\n* fix: keep the site-scope diagnostic off the login decision path\n\nBoth problems were introduced by 53f1df4 and found by a second adversarial pass.\n\n1. That commit made callbacks.siteApplicability() run unconditionally so an\n   enforced factor could LOG when it overrode a site''s group scope. The enforced\n   rows never consult the result - but the call is not free: with\n   <factor>.enabled=true and a non-empty enabledGroups it performs a JCR\n   group-membership read, and on RepositoryException it throws\n   MfaException(internal_error), which propagates out of prepare() and DENIES\n   the sign-in. Before 53f1df4 the `!enforced &&` short-circuit meant this never\n   ran for an enforced factor, so the commit turned a diagnostic into a\n   fail-closed dependency on the group backend for every enforced login on any\n   site that scopes by group. Moving a call out of a short-circuit changes its\n   failure semantics, not only how often it runs.\n\n   The enforced branch now goes through a helper that catches MfaException and\n   drops to DEBUG, so the diagnostic can never fail a login. The warning is also\n   throttled per (factor, user, site) on a 15-minute window through a bounded\n   LRU map - previously it fired on every sign-in of every out-of-scope user,\n   which is precisely the reason given eight lines above for not warning in the\n   disabled-site case.\n\n   The security property is unchanged: a per-site switch may skip an\n   UNCONFIGURED user, never an enrolled one, and an enforced factor still always\n   runs the pick-one rows.\n\n2. resetUserWebauthn never cleared the management lockout, while its TOTP twin\n   does and while the README added in the same commit promised it did. A user\n   locked out of pre-auth registration stayed locked out behind a green `true`.\n   The mutation now clears it, and the test that injected a rate-limiter mock\n   without ever asserting on it now verifies the call.\n\nTests: extensions 198 -> 199, webauthn 57 (an assertion added to an existing\ntest). Both fixes verified red before and green after.\n\nSigned-off-by: Florent BOURASSE <fbourasse@jahia.com>\n\n---------\n\nSigned-off-by: Florent BOURASSE <fbourasse@jahia.com>","tree":{"sha":"67e4ef81dfb4aecf02599671bc81274b3d73c5cb","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Jahia/mfa-community-parent/git/trees/67e4ef81dfb4aecf02599671bc81274b3d73c5cb"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Jahia/mfa-community-parent/git/commits/6f63ec94fa78098070f52214c626fb2c63dd7050","verification":{"payload":"tree 67e4ef81dfb4aecf02599671bc81274b3d73c5cb\nparent 1684358ca7b6087ada68fedcee4bb4fc65a3cb6e\nauthor Florent BOURASSÉ <fbourasse@jahia.com> 1786627672 +0200\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1786627672 +0200\n\nfix(security): a request may never narrow an MFA access-control decision (#100)\n\n* fix(security): a request may never narrow an MFA access-control decision\n\nThree confirmed vulnerabilities, all rooted in trusting a client-supplied\n`site` value, plus the authorization gap it exposed. Each is reproduced by a\nregression test that fails without the fix.\n\n1. CRITICAL - the `site` request parameter defeated the /cms/login gate.\n   MfaLoginGateDecision.isGated() resolved the site from request.getParameter\n   (\"site\"), validated only against [A-Za-z0-9_-]+, and used it to REPLACE the\n   \"is any site enforcing?\" answer. Naming a site where the factor is not\n   enabled turned gating off: a POST to /cms/login carrying valid credentials\n   plus `site=zzznonexistent` authenticated as root with no second factor while\n   enforcedFactors=totp. The site context now only ever WIDENS the decision (it\n   is kept first so an explicitly named enforcing site still bypasses the 60s\n   any-site cache).\n\n2. CRITICAL - the `site` argument to mfaInitiate suppressed a globally\n   enforced factor. MfaEnforcementDecider.prepare() ran per-site applicability\n   BEFORE enforcement, so a caller who had only proven a credential could name a\n   site with the factor disabled, receive a \"skipped\" preparation, and have\n   verify() accept an empty code. Global enforcement is now evaluated first;\n   per-site activation may only opt a NON-enforced factor in.\n\n   Consequence: an enforced factor is now challenged on a site that has it\n   disabled. enrollableFactorsForSite() therefore no longer filters by per-site\n   enablement either - filtering there would answer enrollment_required with an\n   empty offer, trading the bypass for an unrecoverable sign-in. The\n   misconfiguration is logged instead.\n\n3. HIGH - any single-site administrator could strip MFA from any account,\n   including root. resetUserMfa/resetUserWebauthn authorized on siteKey alone\n   and then acted on an unconstrained userId. MfaAdminAccess.requireSiteAdmin\n   ForUser() now authorizes the SUBJECT: a site admin may reset that site''s own\n   users, a global user requires server-administrator rights. Unknown usernames\n   are reported instead of silently returning success.\n\nVerified against a live Jahia 8.2 with the MFA stack deployed: before, (1)\nanswered 302 -> /success.html and (2) left currentUser=root; after, both answer\n403 / factor.totp.invalid_code and the identity stays guest.\n\nTests: 289 -> 319 (extensions 175->194, totp 65->71, webauthn 49->54).\nSigned-off-by: Florent BOURASSE <fbourasse@jahia.com>\n\n* fix(security): close the other half of the site bypass, and stop authorizing on\none user resolution while acting on another\n\nFollow-up to the adversarial review of this PR. The first pass fixed the\nENFORCED path and stopped there; three of the four findings below are the parts\nit missed, and one is a regression it introduced.\n\n1. CRITICAL - the bypass was only half closed. The per-site shell still skipped\n   a factor whenever it was not globally enforced:\n\n       if (!enforced && !callbacks.isSiteApplicable(userId, siteKey))\n           return callbacks.buildSkippedPreparation();\n\n   With `enforcedFactors` empty and the factor merely enabled per site - the\n   module''s documented opt-in - an ENROLLED user is normally challenged, but a\n   caller holding only their password could name a site where the factor is not\n   enabled and receive a skipped preparation, which verify() accepts for any\n   submission including an empty code. The resulting Jahia session is global,\n   not site-scoped, so it is the same full bypass with `enforced` flipped.\n   The rule is now: a per-site switch may skip an UNCONFIGURED user, never an\n   enrolled one.\n\n   The previous pass also added a test asserting the vulnerable behaviour as\n   correct (an enrolled user, site disabled, expected to skip). Rewritten.\n\n2. HIGH - resetUserMfa/resetUserWebauthn authorized the subject with\n   lookupUser(userId, siteKey) (global tree, then the site tree) but acted\n   through the stores, which resolve with lookupUser(userId, session) - global\n   only. Verified against Jahia''s own sources. So every user that can hold MFA\n   in this module is global and was never site-scoped: a site administrator was\n   denied every reset, breaking the documented recovery path; and for a\n   site-scoped user the authorization passed while the write found nothing and\n   returned true, reinstating the silent success this PR set out to remove and\n   clearing lockout counters for an untouched account.\n\n   The subject is now resolved ONCE, with the same global lookup the stores use,\n   and carried into the action. Site-scoped users cannot hold MFA here, so that\n   branch could never succeed and is removed: a reset requires server\n   administration. Rights are checked before existence so the mutation is not a\n   username oracle. Documented in the README.\n\n3. MEDIUM - enrollmentReport(siteKey) gated on site administration but built its\n   report over the whole repository, handing any site administrator the MFA\n   status of every account platform-wide, root included. Now server-admin only;\n   per-site audit events remain site-admin.\n\n4. MEDIUM - per-site group scoping was silently discarded for an enforced\n   factor. Deliberately NOT restored as a decision rule: the group list is\n   per-site data selected by the client-supplied site key, so honouring it would\n   reopen finding 1 one door along - a site administrator could publish\n   enabledGroups excluding the victim, and an attacker would name that site to\n   be released from a platform-wide requirement. Instead the callback is split\n   into a SiteApplicability value carrying enabled/in-scope from one settings\n   snapshot (the same-snapshot invariant is preserved), and an override of an\n   out-of-scope user is now logged with the user, factor, site and the key to\n   change. Behaviour unchanged, documented.\n\nTests: extensions 194 -> 198, totp 71 -> 73, webauthn 54 -> 57. Each finding has\na regression test verified red before the fix and green after; findings 2 and 3\nwere additionally re-verified by restoring the previous implementations and\nwatching exactly the six new tests fail.\n\nSigned-off-by: Florent BOURASSE <fbourasse@jahia.com>\n\n* fix: keep the site-scope diagnostic off the login decision path\n\nBoth problems were introduced by 53f1df4 and found by a second adversarial pass.\n\n1. That commit made callbacks.siteApplicability() run unconditionally so an\n   enforced factor could LOG when it overrode a site''s group scope. The enforced\n   rows never consult the result - but the call is not free: with\n   <factor>.enabled=true and a non-empty enabledGroups it performs a JCR\n   group-membership read, and on RepositoryException it throws\n   MfaException(internal_error), which propagates out of prepare() and DENIES\n   the sign-in. Before 53f1df4 the `!enforced &&` short-circuit meant this never\n   ran for an enforced factor, so the commit turned a diagnostic into a\n   fail-closed dependency on the group backend for every enforced login on any\n   site that scopes by group. Moving a call out of a short-circuit changes its\n   failure semantics, not only how often it runs.\n\n   The enforced branch now goes through a helper that catches MfaException and\n   drops to DEBUG, so the diagnostic can never fail a login. The warning is also\n   throttled per (factor, user, site) on a 15-minute window through a bounded\n   LRU map - previously it fired on every sign-in of every out-of-scope user,\n   which is precisely the reason given eight lines above for not warning in the\n   disabled-site case.\n\n   The security property is unchanged: a per-site switch may skip an\n   UNCONFIGURED user, never an enrolled one, and an enforced factor still always\n   runs the pick-one rows.\n\n2. resetUserWebauthn never cleared the management lockout, while its TOTP twin\n   does and while the README added in the same commit promised it did. A user\n   locked out of pre-auth registration stayed locked out behind a green `true`.\n   The mutation now clears it, and the test that injected a rate-limiter mock\n   without ever asserting on it now verifies the call.\n\nTests: extensions 198 -> 199, webauthn 57 (an assertion added to an existing\ntest). Both fixes verified red before and green after.\n\nSigned-off-by: Florent BOURASSE <fbourasse@jahia.com>\n\n---------\n\nSigned-off-by: Florent BOURASSE <fbourasse@jahia.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqfcZYCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAA4H0QAGZ8QZxHxhkvgPbmr0DZCMGb\n0Ihx9z589T9Gg7Dw5Y/BWbu065mvVCrZ+ZZP2XTD7XhXW7VJA9I2lM4fiKY43Z45\nEl6TfaryGSbd9mFJaURUqgy5GdXe5LNX48dAGO/gVMPZ7ZGMIoUX82wNxpt4Ml4v\nI/xT2x+3vEIkONuTbN0/v76CxKH6YwJi3y58H8VtZnYeV3Vm7P77rOAyAMIFxl7R\nz3TPlbuzlWynRTAuEJc978oR1r2k0Pwb8EaN9WmHulc2+IG2Ygl+vATs9fsFb9SV\nYEcQ1zRWYmvGyiigonD5hQiJFeAiXQY5ajtzBv38rOUA1+72NuI5YFuVIRknFiQA\n/jE/qRczYLjVam3OXI4Zn1yTPHp9nqV0KmRqFnrKBC2aUL9l3pTPgl+sTb+6Xu2H\nvWBNI/sDba1j/mEcL+6PyDxnubeMO9kES1nqzzqqnuz5Uy2VmQqkdl234FoTx8cG\nQCNZAVP1tC6zGp/i1ZbIbEmz/T4AJO0+dXtE565WxIfePWfeFxIYrHShvv2Mtx3c\nnpl8isWCxACklq1lpVzB03o28gB9IaKncp66xoItNyR+3E5/fonxXdYc71WgX9Nw\n3oEAvbMmjac+5PNvLLTy2WP50e/chPzwZTdPMG9FdRvUUrx9opchlSskFek+nKPB\nrV9F2Fgrz1VwHeBBcYxS\n=VVKa\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-08-13T13:33:02Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/Jahia/mfa-community-parent/commit/6f63ec94fa78098070f52214c626fb2c63dd7050","node_id":"C_kwDOSkCe6NoAKDZmNjNlYzk0ZmE3ODA5ODA3MGY1MjIxNGM2MjZmYjJjNjNkZDcwNTA","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/Jahia/mfa-community-parent/commit/1684358ca7b6087ada68fedcee4bb4fc65a3cb6e","sha":"1684358ca7b6087ada68fedcee4bb4fc65a3cb6e","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Jahia/mfa-community-parent/commits/1684358ca7b6087ada68fedcee4bb4fc65a3cb6e"}],"sha":"6f63ec94fa78098070f52214c626fb2c63dd7050","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Jahia/mfa-community-parent/commits/6f63ec94fa78098070f52214c626fb2c63dd7050"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/16238554?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fbourasse/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fbourasse/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fbourasse/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fbourasse/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/fbourasse","id":16238554,"login":"fbourasse","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE2MjM4NTU0","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fbourasse/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fbourasse/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fbourasse/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fbourasse/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/fbourasse/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/fbourasse","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Jahia/mfa-community-parent/commits/1684358ca7b6087ada68fedcee4bb4fc65a3cb6e/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-08-13T12:40:38Z","email":"fbourasse@jahia.com","name":"Florent BOURASSÉ"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-08-13T12:40:38Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"fix: make the login gate''s Authorization: Basic coverage opt-in, plus admin-UI and quality-gate fixes (#101)\n\n* fix: make the login gate''s Authorization: Basic coverage opt-in\n\nPR #98 registers the gate at the head of the authentication pipeline and has it\nrefuse a password presented in an Authorization: Basic header. The placement is\nright, but that credential shape is not confined to /cms/login: it is what every\nnon-interactive client sends, on every endpoint. Gating it unconditionally means\none site enforcing a factor refuses the provisioning API, GraphQL, the tools and\nWebDAV platform-wide - including the API needed to revert the setting, since the\nIP whitelist fails closed behind a reverse proxy (GHSA-4v3g-mcmj-83fp).\n\n- loginGate.gateBasicAuth (default false) arms the header shape. The /cms/login\n  form-parameter block stays unconditional - that bypass must always be closed.\n- A request carrying both shapes answers in the header shape when armed, and\n  falls back to the form redirect when not: the switch must not mask the\n  unconditional block.\n- The per-request block logs at DEBUG with the target URI; a one-shot WARN names\n  the switch and how to revert. A per-request WARN on an unauthenticated path is\n  a log-amplification lever.\n- Documents the switch, its cost and its escape hatch in the shipped .cfg, the\n  README and AGENTS.md, and corrects the stale ''inserted before the\n  password-login valve'' javadoc left by the pipeline-head change.\n- Unit coverage for the opt-in default, both-shapes precedence and hot reload;\n  a new Cypress spec pins both halves of the contract end to end.\n\nSigned-off-by: Florent BOURASSE <fbourasse@jahia.com>\n\n* fix: admin-UI correctness and the missing quality gates\n\nFindings from a blind multi-dimension review of the module (architecture, code\nquality, ergonomics, accessibility, test coverage, documentation).\n\nCorrectness\n- The three admin screens read `useQuery` without destructuring `error`. On a\n  failed load the form kept its constructor defaults and Save wrote them back,\n  so one save during a GraphQL blip silently cleared enforcedFactors, the IP\n  whitelist and the login URL. All three now surface the error, and Save stays\n  disabled until the config has actually populated the form.\n- The admin help text said trusting X-Forwarded-For was ON by default; it is OFF\n  because turning it on makes the whitelist spoofable (SEC-135). The UI also\n  defaulted the checkbox to true and coerced an absent server value to true.\n  Corrected the text and both defaults.\n- The self-service disable/regenerate dialog told users they could enter a backup\n  code. consumeSelfServiceCode is TOTP-only and each attempt burns a rate-limit\n  strike, so a user who lost their authenticator was walked into locking\n  themselves out of their own recovery. The dialog no longer promises it.\n- webauthn''s ResetUserSection had lost the in-flight button label its byte-\n  identical totp twin still has; restored, with the missing locale key.\n\nBuild hygiene\n- `yarn lint` failed in all three webpack modules while `mvn install` passed,\n  because the build never ran it. Wired as a frontend-maven-plugin execution, and\n  the existing violations fixed (indent, capitalized-comments, and one arrow\n  function over the complexity ceiling).\n- No JaCoCo anywhere, so SonarQube reported 0.0% coverage against 235 passing\n  tests. Added per module - not to the root pom, which is an aggregator the\n  modules do not inherit from (they parent to jahia-modules directly) - with\n  surefire argLine late-bound as @{argLine}. Coverage now reports ~66% for\n  extensions and non-zero for the other two.\n- tests/ had no committed lockfile because tests/.gitignore explicitly ignored\n  yarn.lock, so @jahia/cypress floated on every install. Lockfile committed.\n- No CI at all beyond dependabot. Added a build workflow (Temurin 17 - frontend-\n  maven-plugin 2.0.1 will not load on 11 regardless of the Java 11 target)\n  publishing the surefire and jacoco reports.\n\n* revert: withhold the login-gate trigger narrowing pending investigation\n\nThe finding stands and is worth fixing: MfaLoginGateAuthValve gates on the mere\npresence of `username` + `password` request parameters while running at pipeline\nposition 0, so it sees every request, whereas LoginEngineAuthValveImpl reads\nthose parameters only when isLoginRequested() holds (truthy `doLogin`, or\nservletPath /cms + pathInfo /login). Unrelated endpoints that happen to post\nfields with those names are therefore 403''d or redirected while a site enforces\nMFA.\n\nNarrowing the trigger to mirror isLoginRequested() introduced a regression I\ncould not explain, so it is withheld rather than shipped on a security path.\nMeasured on a live Jahia 8.2, enforcement armed, a distinct loginUrl configured,\nPOST /cms/login with credentials and no `doLogin`:\n\n  with the narrowing:     HTTP 500, Location: /sites/<site>/login.html?redirect=...\n  without it:             HTTP 302, same Location\n  with the narrowing\n    + doLogin=true:       HTTP 302, same Location\n\njahia.log shows `IllegalStateException: Cannot call sendRedirect() after the\nresponse has been committed` for the 500 cases. The valve short-circuits the\nauth PIPELINE but JcrSessionFilter continues the SERVLET chain regardless, so\nJahia''s own login handling redirects a second time over the committed response.\nThat double-redirect is structural and pre-existing; what I cannot account for\nis why the gate decision - identical in both builds, and verified identical -\nchanges the downstream outcome. http.loginGate.cy.ts:187 fails on 500 with the\nnarrowing and passes without it (confirmed in isolation, not cross-spec noise).\n\nSecurity was NOT affected either way: with the narrowing deployed, a POST of\nvalid root credentials to /cms/login with no `doLogin` still answered 403 and\nleft the session as guest, so the /cms/login gate never reopened.\n\nReverting MfaLoginGateAuthValve and its test to the previous commit. The\nover-matching finding, the reproduction above, and the servlet-chain\ndouble-redirect it exposed should be fixed together in a dedicated change.\n\nSigned-off-by: Florent BOURASSE <fbourasse@jahia.com>\n\n* fix: stop the gate writing the response twice, and restore the trigger narrowing\n\nThe 500 that commit 3d60f23 could not explain has a cause, and it is ours.\n\nMfaLoginGateAuthValve short-circuits the AUTH PIPELINE when it blocks - it writes\nthe response and returns without invokeNext. But JcrSessionFilter continues the\nSERVLET chain regardless of what the pipeline decided, so MfaLoginGateFilter -\nbound to /cms/login, gating purely on isGated() + isClientWhitelisted() and never\nlooking at credentials - then re-evaluated the SAME decision and wrote a second\nterminal response onto the committed one:\n\n  java.lang.IllegalStateException: Cannot call sendRedirect() after the response\n  has been committed\n\nwhich reaches the client as 500 with an otherwise correct Location header. The two\ncomponents already shared MfaLoginGateDecision; what was missing was not a second\ndecision but a signal that a block had already happened.\n\nThe valve now sets a request attribute the moment it short-circuits, before writing\nanything. The filter honours that attribute, and checks response.isCommitted() as a\nsecond line that also covers any other upstream committer. The GET case - no\ncredentials, so the valve never reaches its block branch - is untouched, which is\nthe filter''s remaining purpose. The coupling is written into the javadoc of both\nclasses.\n\nWith the double write closed, the trigger narrowing withheld in 3d60f23 is restored\n(along with the redundant equals/hashCode removal from the same commit): the gate\nagain mirrors LoginEngineAuthValveImpl#isLoginRequested - truthy `doLogin`, or\nservletPath /cms with pathInfo /login - so it no longer 403s unrelated endpoints\nthat merely post fields named username and password while a site enforces MFA.\n\nVerified on a live Jahia 8.2, POST /cms/login with credentials and no doLogin:\n\n  reverted state (no narrowing)      302\n  narrowing alone                    500   <- the reported regression, reproduced\n  double-write fix + narrowing       302   <- resolved, no IllegalStateException\n\nE2E unchanged and green: http.loginGate 11/11, http.basicAuthGate 8/8, including\nthe spec that previously hit the 500. Unit tests 189 -> 198.\n\nSigned-off-by: Florent BOURASSE <fbourasse@jahia.com>\n\n* fix(ci): quote a step name so the workflow parses\n\nThe step name contained ''install: compiles'', and the unquoted colon-space made\nYAML read it as a nested mapping. GitHub rejected the whole file: the run was\nnamed after the file path instead of the workflow, produced zero jobs, and\nreported failure - so the CI added in 522335b never actually ran a build.\n\nValidated with yaml.safe_load: one job, five steps, push and pull_request\ntriggers.\n\nSigned-off-by: Florent BOURASSE <fbourasse@jahia.com>\n\n---------\n\nSigned-off-by: Florent BOURASSE <fbourasse@jahia.com>","tree":{"sha":"dc1a39cf373b491dce7ae8dd77ab797ba78af8ea","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Jahia/mfa-community-parent/git/trees/dc1a39cf373b491dce7ae8dd77ab797ba78af8ea"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/Jahia/mfa-community-parent/git/commits/1684358ca7b6087ada68fedcee4bb4fc65a3cb6e","verification":{"payload":"tree dc1a39cf373b491dce7ae8dd77ab797ba78af8ea\nparent be96a08190b4625d8f81b2ff09ee6110200ab444\nauthor Florent BOURASSÉ <fbourasse@jahia.com> 1786624838 +0200\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1786624838 +0200\n\nfix: make the login gate''s Authorization: Basic coverage opt-in, plus admin-UI and quality-gate fixes (#101)\n\n* fix: make the login gate''s Authorization: Basic coverage opt-in\n\nPR #98 registers the gate at the head of the authentication pipeline and has it\nrefuse a password presented in an Authorization: Basic header. The placement is\nright, but that credential shape is not confined to /cms/login: it is what every\nnon-interactive client sends, on every endpoint. Gating it unconditionally means\none site enforcing a factor refuses the provisioning API, GraphQL, the tools and\nWebDAV platform-wide - including the API needed to revert the setting, since the\nIP whitelist fails closed behind a reverse proxy (GHSA-4v3g-mcmj-83fp).\n\n- loginGate.gateBasicAuth (default false) arms the header shape. The /cms/login\n  form-parameter block stays unconditional - that bypass must always be closed.\n- A request carrying both shapes answers in the header shape when armed, and\n  falls back to the form redirect when not: the switch must not mask the\n  unconditional block.\n- The per-request block logs at DEBUG with the target URI; a one-shot WARN names\n  the switch and how to revert. A per-request WARN on an unauthenticated path is\n  a log-amplification lever.\n- Documents the switch, its cost and its escape hatch in the shipped .cfg, the\n  README and AGENTS.md, and corrects the stale ''inserted before the\n  password-login valve'' javadoc left by the pipeline-head change.\n- Unit coverage for the opt-in default, both-shapes precedence and hot reload;\n  a new Cypress spec pins both halves of the contract end to end.\n\nSigned-off-by: Florent BOURASSE <fbourasse@jahia.com>\n\n* fix: admin-UI correctness and the missing quality gates\n\nFindings from a blind multi-dimension review of the module (architecture, code\nquality, ergonomics, accessibility, test coverage, documentation).\n\nCorrectness\n- The three admin screens read `useQuery` without destructuring `error`. On a\n  failed load the form kept its constructor defaults and Save wrote them back,\n  so one save during a GraphQL blip silently cleared enforcedFactors, the IP\n  whitelist and the login URL. All three now surface the error, and Save stays\n  disabled until the config has actually populated the form.\n- The admin help text said trusting X-Forwarded-For was ON by default; it is OFF\n  because turning it on makes the whitelist spoofable (SEC-135). The UI also\n  defaulted the checkbox to true and coerced an absent server value to true.\n  Corrected the text and both defaults.\n- The self-service disable/regenerate dialog told users they could enter a backup\n  code. consumeSelfServiceCode is TOTP-only and each attempt burns a rate-limit\n  strike, so a user who lost their authenticator was walked into locking\n  themselves out of their own recovery. The dialog no longer promises it.\n- webauthn''s ResetUserSection had lost the in-flight button label its byte-\n  identical totp twin still has; restored, with the missing locale key.\n\nBuild hygiene\n- `yarn lint` failed in all three webpack modules while `mvn install` passed,\n  because the build never ran it. Wired as a frontend-maven-plugin execution, and\n  the existing violations fixed (indent, capitalized-comments, and one arrow\n  function over the complexity ceiling).\n- No JaCoCo anywhere, so SonarQube reported 0.0% coverage against 235 passing\n  tests. Added per module - not to the root pom, which is an aggregator the\n  modules do not inherit from (they parent to jahia-modules directly) - with\n  surefire argLine late-bound as @{argLine}. Coverage now reports ~66% for\n  extensions and non-zero for the other two.\n- tests/ had no committed lockfile because tests/.gitignore explicitly ignored\n  yarn.lock, so @jahia/cypress floated on every install. Lockfile committed.\n- No CI at all beyond dependabot. Added a build workflow (Temurin 17 - frontend-\n  maven-plugin 2.0.1 will not load on 11 regardless of the Java 11 target)\n  publishing the surefire and jacoco reports.\n\n* revert: withhold the login-gate trigger narrowing pending investigation\n\nThe finding stands and is worth fixing: MfaLoginGateAuthValve gates on the mere\npresence of `username` + `password` request parameters while running at pipeline\nposition 0, so it sees every request, whereas LoginEngineAuthValveImpl reads\nthose parameters only when isLoginRequested() holds (truthy `doLogin`, or\nservletPath /cms + pathInfo /login). Unrelated endpoints that happen to post\nfields with those names are therefore 403''d or redirected while a site enforces\nMFA.\n\nNarrowing the trigger to mirror isLoginRequested() introduced a regression I\ncould not explain, so it is withheld rather than shipped on a security path.\nMeasured on a live Jahia 8.2, enforcement armed, a distinct loginUrl configured,\nPOST /cms/login with credentials and no `doLogin`:\n\n  with the narrowing:     HTTP 500, Location: /sites/<site>/login.html?redirect=...\n  without it:             HTTP 302, same Location\n  with the narrowing\n    + doLogin=true:       HTTP 302, same Location\n\njahia.log shows `IllegalStateException: Cannot call sendRedirect() after the\nresponse has been committed` for the 500 cases. The valve short-circuits the\nauth PIPELINE but JcrSessionFilter continues the SERVLET chain regardless, so\nJahia''s own login handling redirects a second time over the committed response.\nThat double-redirect is structural and pre-existing; what I cannot account for\nis why the gate decision - identical in both builds, and verified identical -\nchanges the downstream outcome. http.loginGate.cy.ts:187 fails on 500 with the\nnarrowing and passes without it (confirmed in isolation, not cross-spec noise).\n\nSecurity was NOT affected either way: with the narrowing deployed, a POST of\nvalid root credentials to /cms/login with no `doLogin` still answered 403 and\nleft the session as guest, so the /cms/login gate never reopened.\n\nReverting MfaLoginGateAuthValve and its test to the previous commit. The\nover-matching finding, the reproduction above, and the servlet-chain\ndouble-redirect it exposed should be fixed together in a dedicated change.\n\nSigned-off-by: Florent BOURASSE <fbourasse@jahia.com>\n\n* fix: stop the gate writing the response twice, and restore the trigger narrowing\n\nThe 500 that commit 3d60f23 could not explain has a cause, and it is ours.\n\nMfaLoginGateAuthValve short-circuits the AUTH PIPELINE when it blocks - it writes\nthe response and returns without invokeNext. But JcrSessionFilter continues the\nSERVLET chain regardless of what the pipeline decided, so MfaLoginGateFilter -\nbound to /cms/login, gating purely on isGated() + isClientWhitelisted() and never\nlooking at credentials - then re-evaluated the SAME decision and wrote a second\nterminal response onto the committed one:\n\n  java.lang.IllegalStateException: Cannot call sendRedirect() after the response\n  has been committed\n\nwhich reaches the client as 500 with an otherwise correct Location header. The two\ncomponents already shared MfaLoginGateDecision; what was missing was not a second\ndecision but a signal that a block had already happened.\n\nThe valve now sets a request attribute the moment it short-circuits, before writing\nanything. The filter honours that attribute, and checks response.isCommitted() as a\nsecond line that also covers any other upstream committer. The GET case - no\ncredentials, so the valve never reaches its block branch - is untouched, which is\nthe filter''s remaining purpose. The coupling is written into the javadoc of both\nclasses.\n\nWith the double write closed, the trigger narrowing withheld in 3d60f23 is restored\n(along with the redundant equals/hashCode removal from the same commit): the gate\nagain mirrors LoginEngineAuthValveImpl#isLoginRequested - truthy `doLogin`, or\nservletPath /cms with pathInfo /login - so it no longer 403s unrelated endpoints\nthat merely post fields named username and password while a site enforces MFA.\n\nVerified on a live Jahia 8.2, POST /cms/login with credentials and no doLogin:\n\n  reverted state (no narrowing)      302\n  narrowing alone                    500   <- the reported regression, reproduced\n  double-write fix + narrowing       302   <- resolved, no IllegalStateException\n\nE2E unchanged and green: http.loginGate 11/11, http.basicAuthGate 8/8, including\nthe spec that previously hit the 500. Unit tests 189 -> 198.\n\nSigned-off-by: Florent BOURASSE <fbourasse@jahia.com>\n\n* fix(ci): quote a step name so the workflow parses\n\nThe step name contained ''install: compiles'', and the unquoted colon-space made\nYAML read it as a nested mapping. GitHub rejected the whole file: the run was\nnamed after the file path instead of the workflow, produced zero jobs, and\nreported failure - so the CI added in 522335b never actually ran a build.\n\nValidated with yaml.safe_load: one job, five steps, push and pull_request\ntriggers.\n\nSigned-off-by: Florent BOURASSE <fbourasse@jahia.com>\n\n---------\n\nSigned-off-by: Florent BOURASSE <fbourasse@jahia.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqfbtGCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAfHcQACI5rqP0XtY